<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Mpondo Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1894 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Pondoland on the Wild Coast, the last independent African state south of Natal to be annexed by the Cape in 1894.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpondo">Mpondo</a> were a southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> people whose royal genealogy reaches back many generations, settled between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mthatha-river">Mtata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtamvuna-river">Mtamvuna</a> rivers by the 17th century. The Mpondo grew powerful in the early 19th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faku">Faku</a>, who survived raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaka-zulu-king">Shaka</a>'s <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a> and absorbed refugees from the wars of that era.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled through royal kin and councillors over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-keeping and farming homesteads, and the Great Place was the centre of justice and ritual. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faku">Faku</a> signed a treaty with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in 1844 recognising his rule over a large territory. After his death in 1867 the kingdom divided into Eastern Pondoland under Mqikela and Western Pondoland under Ndamase.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> pressed for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-st-johns">Port St Johns</a> and the Pondoland coast. In 1894 Mqikela's successor Sigcawu was compelled to accept annexation to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. Pondoland was the scene of a major revolt against apartheid policies in 1960.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1600 CE – 1894 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:5.85%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1894 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Faku, Mqikela, Sigcawu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Xhosa religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>isiXhosa (Mpondo)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pondoland, amaMpondo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1747&amp;focus=mpondo-kingdom" aria-label="Mpondo Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5306 3256 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mpondo Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5470 3663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5626 3512h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5626 3512h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day South Africa. <a href="/atlas?year=1747&amp;focus=mpondo-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
